ased or raised so there are areas  where they are having a real impact but  the public doesn't want to focus on that  and I think the NFL is smart and saying  we feel these these issues are important  we want to continue to work with our  players on that we want to show them  that we're doing the right thing that we  do support them not only in money but  you have owners who have been out you  know doing these things some of the more  progressive owners in this when you talk  about it you talk about a Jed York you  talk about a Michael Bidwell who's been  really supportive Arthur Blank shad Kahn  Stephen Ross Jeffrey Lurie you can go  down the list who these guys say this  these issues that these players are  fighting for are very important to us  and we do support them on this we also  understand that there is a business  element to this in terms of how some  fans feel and we also want to be  respectful of that well then I guess the  last one for you then Jim is is there a  group of players who based on what you  just said who are who would basically  not sit knowing that it conflates the  issue for people that will never  understand why they're sitting or  kneeling and taking away the attention  of all that good work that you're  talking about that if you posted all the  focus on that that this is where the  social injustice correction comes from  as opposed to kneeling is there is that  there's some sort of movement there that  that that the kneeling kind of run its  course if you will you know well that  was the feeling of did I mean Jed felt  that it had run its course again when  you look at the end of last season there  were only a handful of players who were  kneeling and so he  felt that they were they were buying  that and I go back to the words of dr.  Harry Edwards it was a piece I wrote at  the end of last season where dr. Harry  Edwards the renowned sports psychologist  and and an activist it said movements  are fleeting and he talked about back in  th